DIRECTIVE_ID: KOL-BOOST-011
TITLE: Machine-Readable Kolibri Brain
PRIORITY: P0
MODE: APPLY_IMMEDIATELY_NO_WAIT

This directive extends all previously received owner directives.

Do not stop, pause, cancel, or duplicate existing work.

Continue:

- remote factory operation;
- network convergence;
- node repairs;
- Telegram Mini App;
- client application development;
- active pull requests;
- current root workflows.

Do not use internal Codex subagents as production workers.

All implementation must be performed by real remote agents through the
existing Kolibri Factory Control Plane.

======================================================================
1. PRIMARY OBJECTIVE
======================================================================

Build a machine-readable central brain for Kolibri.

Every owner instruction received through:

- Codex App;
- Telegram;
- Kolibri Web UI;
- Control API;
- future ChatGPT integration;

must be automatically converted from natural language into a validated,
versioned, machine-readable execution contract.

Required pipeline:

owner natural-language instruction
→ immutable raw directive
→ structured directive
→ schema validation
→ dependency detection
→ conflict detection
→ policy evaluation
→ root goal
→ task graph
→ scheduling
→ execution
→ evidence
→ verified lesson
→ shared knowledge

The owner must not manually create YAML, JSON, branches, servers, or task
dependencies.

The system must translate owner intent into machine-readable structures
automatically.

======================================================================
2. DO NOT BUILD A SINGLE FRAGILE BRAIN CONTAINER
======================================================================

“Kolibri Brain” is one logical service, but it must not be one physical
single point of failure.

Deploy the brain as cooperating services:

- kolibri-directive-compiler;
- kolibri-brain-api;
- kolibri-workflow-service;
- kolibri-knowledge-service;
- kolibri-policy-service;
- kolibri-artifact-service;
- kolibri-event-service;
- kolibri-reconciler;
- kolibri-observability.

Primary runs the active Control Plane.

Home runs:

- active compute;
- knowledge replica;
- artifact replica;
- standby Control Plane.

A third stable node acts as consensus/witness where required.

Do not introduce a hypervisor as a mandatory dependency.

Use:

- OCI containers;
- systemd;
- Nomad scheduling;
- isolated workspaces;
- resource limits.

Use virtual machines only for tasks requiring stronger isolation than
containers can provide.

======================================================================
3. DIRECTIVE STORAGE
======================================================================

Create:

.factory/directives/
  raw/
  compiled/
  active/
  superseded/
  failed/

.factory/contracts/
  directive.schema.json
  root-goal.schema.json
  task.schema.json
  task-attempt.schema.json
  node.schema.json
  agent.schema.json
  policy-decision.schema.json
  proposal.schema.json
  review.schema.json
  vote.schema.json
  artifact.schema.json
  lesson.schema.json
  evidence.schema.json

.factory/memory/
  directive-ledger.jsonl
  decision-ledger.jsonl
  conflict-ledger.jsonl
  lesson-ledger.jsonl

Every directive must store:

- directive_id;
- source;
- owner_id;
- raw_text;
- language;
- received_at;
- sha256;
- priority;
- mode;
- dependencies;
- supersedes;
- conflicts;
- objectives;
- constraints;
- acceptance_criteria;
- budgets;
- risk_level;
- execution_policy;
- created_root_goals;
- current_status;
- evidence_refs;
- artifact_refs.

The raw owner text is immutable.

Never overwrite an old directive.

A corrected directive creates a new version and marks the old version
superseded.

======================================================================
4. DIRECTIVE COMPILER
======================================================================

Create service:

kolibri-directive-compiler.service

API:

POST /v1/directives
GET  /v1/directives
GET  /v1/directives/{directive_id}
POST /v1/directives/{directive_id}/compile
POST /v1/directives/{directive_id}/activate
POST /v1/directives/{directive_id}/pause
POST /v1/directives/{directive_id}/supersede
GET  /v1/directives/{directive_id}/evidence

Compilation stages:

1. Save raw instruction.
2. Detect language.
3. Extract owner intent.
4. Identify objectives.
5. Identify constraints.
6. Identify acceptance criteria.
7. Identify required capabilities.
8. Identify dependencies.
9. Identify conflicts with active directives.
10. Select execution mode:
    - SIMPLE;
    - MEDIUM;
    - COUNCIL.
11. Estimate task count and resources.
12. Apply policy.
13. Generate RootGoal.
14. Generate initial task DAG.
15. Validate every document against JSON Schema.
16. Calculate SHA-256.
17. Store compiled contract.
18. Register it in Control Plane.
19. Emit an event.
20. Start execution immediately.

The language model may propose structured output, but unvalidated output must
never enter execution.

Required validation:

- JSON Schema;
- semantic validators;
- budget validator;
- recursion validator;
- permission validator;
- dependency validator;
- conflict validator;
- duplicate detector.

Invalid directives must not silently disappear.

They receive:

- INVALID;
- NEEDS_REPAIR;
- or BLOCKED;

with an exact reason.

======================================================================
5. AUTOMATIC CONVERSION OF EXISTING INSTRUCTIONS
======================================================================

Discover all existing instructions from:

- AGENTS.md;
- AGENTS.override.md;
- MASTER_DIRECTIVE.md;
- .factory/directives;
- .factory/policies;
- .factory/orders;
- .factory/root-goals;
- docs/runbooks;
- active task artifacts;
- owner directives in the current project history.

Convert all active instructions into machine-readable contracts.

Do not treat every old document as active.

Classify each as:

- ACTIVE;
- SUPERSEDED;
- HISTORICAL;
- CONFLICTING;
- INFORMATIONAL;
- INVALID.

Build:

.factory/manifests/directive-migration.json

For every migrated directive record:

- source file;
- original SHA-256;
- assigned directive ID;
- classification;
- current replacement;
- conflicts;
- activation status.

Do not delete historical instructions.

======================================================================
6. ROOT GOALS AND TASK GRAPH
======================================================================

Every activated directive creates one or more RootGoals.

RootGoal must contain:

- root_goal_id;
- directive_id;
- objective;
- acceptance criteria;
- priority;
- current phase;
- task graph;
- budgets;
- deadline;
- owner controls;
- evidence;
- final result.

Tasks form a directed acyclic graph.

Every task contains:

- root_task_id;
- task_id;
- parent_task_id;
- dependencies;
- objective;
- acceptance criteria;
- task_type;
- required capabilities;
- assigned agent;
- assigned node;
- budget;
- recursion depth;
- idempotency key;
- lease;
- heartbeat;
- state;
- attempts;
- artifacts;
- evidence.

Default recursion limits:

max_depth: 5
max_children_per_task: 20
max_active_children: 10
max_retries: 3

The compiler may create additional tasks during execution when:

- a bug is discovered;
- a test fails;
- a dependency is missing;
- a reviewer requests changes;
- a node fails;
- research identifies a required compatibility task.

All generated tasks must preserve root_goal_id and provenance.

======================================================================
7. CENTRAL KNOWLEDGE BRAIN
======================================================================

Create:

kolibri-knowledge-service.service

Use:

- PostgreSQL for structured records;
- JSONB for versioned flexible documents;
- pgvector for semantic retrieval;
- MinIO/S3-compatible storage for large artifacts.

Store structured knowledge:

- directives;
- tasks;
- task attempts;
- agents;
- nodes;
- decisions;
- reviews;
- votes;
- test reports;
- incidents;
- performance benchmarks;
- user feedback;
- architecture decisions;
- verified lessons;
- rejected approaches;
- official research sources.

Large artifacts remain in object storage:

- source archives;
- build outputs;
- screenshots;
- PDFs;
- documents;
- datasets;
- FormulaLM bundles;
- logs;
- trace exports;
- release packages.

PostgreSQL stores artifact metadata and SHA-256 references.

Create internal endpoints:

brain.kolibri.internal
knowledge.kolibri.internal
artifacts.kolibri.internal

Agents must retrieve relevant VERIFIED knowledge before starting a task.

Unreviewed notes must not be treated as verified truth.

======================================================================
8. KNOWLEDGE STATUS
======================================================================

Every knowledge item has a state:

DRAFT
OBSERVED
REVIEWED
VERIFIED
REJECTED
SUPERSEDED
REVOKED

Knowledge becomes VERIFIED only after:

- source provenance;
- evidence;
- independent review;
- passing tests where applicable;
- reproducibility;
- versioning.

Every completed task generates a lesson artifact:

- task objective;
- implementation summary;
- selected approach;
- rejected alternatives;
- evidence;
- test results;
- failures;
- regression tests;
- reusable patterns;
- limitations;
- source commits;
- PR;
- artifact hashes.

Agents may use VERIFIED knowledge automatically.

Agents may use DRAFT knowledge only as a hypothesis requiring verification.

======================================================================
9. EVENT AND MESSAGE LAYER
======================================================================

Introduce NATS JetStream as the persistent event and communication layer.

Do not immediately remove the existing Redis queue.

Migration mode:

- existing Redis remains operational;
- NATS receives mirrored events;
- verify correctness;
- move selected low-risk task classes;
- perform failure tests;
- migrate only after PASS;
- maintain rollback.

Create streams:

KOLIBRI_DIRECTIVES
KOLIBRI_GOALS
KOLIBRI_TASKS
KOLIBRI_RESULTS
KOLIBRI_REVIEWS
KOLIBRI_EVENTS
KOLIBRI_INCIDENTS
KOLIBRI_LESSONS

Use durable pull consumers.

Every event includes:

- event_id;
- event_type;
- trace_id;
- directive_id;
- root_goal_id;
- task_id;
- attempt_id;
- agent_id;
- node_id;
- timestamp;
- schema_version;
- payload_ref;
- idempotency_key.

Configure:

- explicit acknowledgements;
- retry policy;
- dead-letter handling;
- retention limits;
- stream size limits;
- replication factor appropriate to node health.

Do not allow streams or logs to grow without limits.

======================================================================
10. DURABLE WORKFLOW LAYER
======================================================================

Introduce Temporal incrementally.

Do not replace the currently working Factory Runtime in one destructive
migration.

Use Temporal first for new RootGoals and long-running orchestration:

- directive execution;
- recursive task trees;
- swarm councils;
- review cycles;
- staging and release;
- node repair;
- disaster recovery;
- periodic reconciliation.

Existing task execution may continue through the current Control Plane until
Temporal canary passes.

Migration:

1. Start Temporal side-by-side.
2. Implement one canary RootGoal.
3. Test process restart.
4. Test server restart.
5. Test network interruption.
6. Test worker recovery.
7. Verify zero lost tasks.
8. Verify zero duplicate effects.
9. Migrate additional workflows gradually.

Temporal must store IDs and references.

Do not store large PDFs, source archives, or model files directly in workflow
history.

======================================================================
11. SCHEDULER AND CONTAINER EXECUTION
======================================================================

Introduce Nomad as the distributed workload scheduler.

Do not require the owner to select nodes manually.

Nomad server nodes:

- Primary;
- Home;
- one stable witness.

All healthy servers are Nomad clients.

Schedule:

- Agent Hosts;
- isolated task executors;
- browser tests;
- builds;
- model workers;
- FormulaLM jobs;
- PDF generation;
- research workers;
- repair workers.

Each job declares:

- CPU;
- RAM;
- disk;
- GPU;
- network;
- models;
- tools;
- capabilities;
- priority;
- deadline;
- node constraints;
- max runtime;
- retry policy.

Use OCI containers where appropriate.

Use local isolated worktrees for code tasks.

Do not place active multi-writer Git worktrees on shared network storage.

======================================================================
12. NO ROUTINE SSH EXECUTION
======================================================================

After bootstrap, ordinary Factory operation must not use SSH.

Workers must:

1. start as persistent services;
2. connect outbound to the Control Plane/event layer;
3. register capabilities;
4. receive tasks from the queue;
5. upload results to artifact storage;
6. update task state;
7. maintain heartbeat.

SSH is permitted only for:

- initial provisioning;
- repair;
- emergency break-glass access;
- recovery of a completely disconnected node.

Create OPA policy:

routine_task_execution_via_ssh = deny

Before any routine SSH action, the system must prove:

- node agent unavailable;
- internal network unavailable;
- repair task exists;
- break-glass reason recorded.

Remove public SSH addresses from normal Agent Host configuration.

======================================================================
13. POLICY ENGINE
======================================================================

Create:

kolibri-policy-service.service

Use OPA policies for:

- who may create tasks;
- which agents may use tools;
- filesystem access;
- network access;
- model access;
- secret access;
- node placement;
- staging deployment;
- production deployment;
- destructive actions;
- budget changes;
- recursive task limits;
- swarm mode selection;
- public SSH fallback.

Default policy:

deny unless explicitly allowed.

Policy decisions must be stored with:

- input;
- policy version;
- result;
- reason;
- timestamp;
- trace_id.

Never store secrets in policy decision logs.

======================================================================
14. FULL OBSERVABILITY
======================================================================

Instrument all services with OpenTelemetry.

Every operation must include:

- trace_id;
- span_id;
- directive_id;
- root_goal_id;
- task_id;
- attempt_id;
- agent_id;
- node_id;
- model;
- tool;
- tokens;
- cost;
- latency;
- state;
- artifact refs.

Collect:

- traces;
- metrics;
- structured logs.

Provide dashboards for:

- directive compilation;
- active root goals;
- task queue;
- agents;
- nodes;
- failures;
- retries;
- leases;
- swarm voting;
- knowledge ingestion;
- deployment;
- cost;
- network;
- disk.

No claim may be displayed without evidence.

======================================================================
15. 15-MINUTE AUTONOMIC RECONCILIATION
======================================================================

Create:

kolibri-reconciler.service
kolibri-reconciler.timer

Interval:

15 minutes

Every cycle checks:

- active directives;
- directive conflicts;
- root goals;
- blocked tasks;
- stale leases;
- agent heartbeats;
- node health;
- network connectivity;
- disk and inode usage;
- service health;
- pull requests;
- CI;
- staging;
- production;
- backups;
- artifacts;
- knowledge ingestion;
- unresolved incidents;
- budget use.

The reconciler may automatically:

- retry safe tasks;
- release stale leases;
- drain unhealthy nodes;
- quarantine critical nodes;
- create repair tasks;
- restart Kolibri services;
- clean expired safe artifacts;
- resume paused workflows after allowed recovery;
- update Telegram status.

It must not automatically:

- delete user data;
- format disks;
- publish secrets;
- purchase resources;
- make irreversible migrations without rollback.

Every reconciliation cycle creates a signed report artifact.

======================================================================
16. TELEGRAM NOTIFICATIONS
======================================================================

Do not wait for owner acknowledgement after accepting a directive.

Immediately send asynchronously:

DIRECTIVE_ACCEPTED
ID: <directive_id>
COMPILED: true/false
ROOT_GOALS: <ids>
TASKS_CREATED: <count>
STATUS: executing
BLOCKERS: <only concrete blockers>

Continue execution immediately.

Send additional Telegram messages only for:

- remote handoff ready;
- major phase completed;
- owner decision required;
- critical incident;
- task completed;
- deployment completed;
- STOP/START status.

Do not send raw heartbeat noise.

======================================================================
17. MIGRATION SAFETY
======================================================================

Do not replace all existing infrastructure at once.

Use side-by-side canaries.

Required migration order:

1. Directive schemas and compiler.
2. Directive ledger.
3. PostgreSQL/pgvector knowledge service.
4. Artifact service.
5. NATS event mirroring.
6. Temporal canary workflow.
7. Nomad canary task executor.
8. OPA policy canary.
9. OpenTelemetry instrumentation.
10. Gradual workload migration.
11. Routine SSH removal.
12. HA and failover tests.

Every stage requires:

- backup;
- test;
- canary;
- evidence;
- rollback;
- zero lost tasks;
- zero duplicate effects.

Existing Telegram Mini App and client application work must continue during
migration.

======================================================================
18. ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
======================================================================

PASS only when:

- every new owner instruction is automatically persisted;
- every instruction is compiled to validated machine-readable contracts;
- every compiled contract creates traceable RootGoals and tasks;
- all old active directives are migrated and classified;
- the Knowledge Brain is operational;
- agents retrieve verified relevant knowledge;
- NATS event layer is operational;
- Temporal canary survives restart and network failure;
- Nomad schedules a real remote task;
- OPA blocks an unauthorized action;
- OpenTelemetry shows an end-to-end trace;
- ordinary Factory tasks no longer use SSH;
- the 15-minute reconciler works;
- Telegram receives directive acceptance and completion;
- existing Factory tasks continue;
- existing Mini App work continues;
- lost tasks = 0;
- duplicate effects = 0;
- rollback has been tested.

